    duration: "136 min",
    artworkUrl: "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Podcasts221/v4/aa/f1/51/aaf151f6-8661-833a-c9d3-7c4ce22f8868/mza_253061105143942369.jpg/600x600bb.jpg",
    podcastName: "Huberman Lab",
    hosts: "Andrew Huberman",
    tldl: `Women and men respond similarly to exercise, debunking the myth that women require different training protocols. Dr. Lauren Colenso-Semple emphasizes evidence-based approaches to fitness that are relevant for all genders.`,
    whatHappened: `The dialogue further explored societal changes in perceptions of women's strength training. Dr. Colenso-Semple noted that the rise of competitive categories like bikini bodybuilding and group fitness classes has empowered women to embrace weightlifting without fear of becoming 'bulky.' This shift has fostered a community where women feel more confident in pursuing resistance training, and she argued that the notion of needing to 'tone' without gaining size is misleading. In closing, the episode reinforced the idea that effective training strategies are universally applicable, regardless of gender.`,
    keyInsights: [`Women and men have similar responses to exercise, highlighting the importance of inclusive fitness programming.`, `Muscle protein synthesis occurs similarly in both genders, debunking the need for gender-specific training protocols.`, `Acute hormonal responses to exercise do not predict long-term muscle growth, shifting the focus to consistent training over maximizing short-term hormonal spikes.`, `Cultural shifts, such as the popularity of bodybuilding categories and group fitness, have empowered women to engage more confidently in weightlifting.`],
    quote: `The narrative is very much, women are not men. And so obviously women need something different. The data says men and women respond to exercise very similarly.`,
    quoteAttribution: `Dr. Lauren Colenso-Semple`,
