    whatHappened: `The episode kicks off with Shawn Ryan welcoming Wes Huff, a Canadian Christian apologist who has an intriguing background, including growing up in Pakistan and overcoming a rare childhood condition. They bond over shared interests and gifts, with Shawn presenting Wes with a unique Roman dagger and a piece of a 1576 Geneva Bible, emphasizing the historical depth of scripture. This sets the stage for a deeper conversation about faith and its implications in a world filled with suffering and evil.

As the discussion unfolds, Shawn opens up about his internal struggles with faith, particularly in light of the atrocities he frequently encounters in the news. He questions the point of living as a Christian when one could simply profess faith at the end of life and still gain entry into heaven. Wes responds thoughtfully, addressing the problem of evil and the nature of God, emphasizing that the existence of evil points to a moral law that requires a moral lawgiver. He articulates the unique Christian perspective on suffering, explaining that God is not distant from human pain but rather intimately connected to it through the life and sacrifice of Jesus, who experienced suffering himself.

Wes further elaborates on the relationship between humanity and God, using the analogy of separation from one's Creator leading to death, much like fish out of water. He underscores that while the world remains broken, the promise of renewal and eternal life through Christ offers hope. The conversation concludes on a poignant note, reflecting the complexities of faith, morality, and the assurance of divine compassion amidst human suffering.`,
